WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.630
Episode 5 of The Last of Us has a perfect
blend of deviations and faithfulness to the

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:05.630
source material.

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:10.000
It does enough new to completely enhance the
original while not going too far to the point

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.059
of making it unrecognizable.

00:00:12.059 --> 00:00:16.390
Episode 3 was my favorite, but I can see why
some people may dislike that episode because

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:21.470
of how much of a departure it was from the
game, among other reasons too.

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:26.160
But episode 5 I feel like will gain the mass
appeal because the changes that were made

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.119
were absolutely insane.

00:00:28.119 --> 00:00:32.279
The entire action setpiece that resembles
hardhome from Game of Thrones was basically

00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:34.040
entirely original.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:37.890
Besides the sniper standoff, that was the
one shared aspect.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:42.750
So first let’s talk about these big changes
and how genius it was overall because I think

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.520
this episode is an improvement over the game.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.840
Action wise the undead massacre is such an
adrenaline fueling moment that I was in shock

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.309
about how amazing this scene was.

00:00:52.309 --> 00:00:56.420
From the spectacle of the explosion to then
revealing the sinkhole that is perfectly timed

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:59.420
writing-wise to coincide with the encounter
with Henry.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:04.820
The gradual oh shit moment that slowly builds
as we hear the infected thundering footsteps

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:06.610
approach our characters was great.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:11.530
Also, I love how the sound drops out just
before they emerge, it gives off this erupting

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:20.079
feeling along with having some orgasmic payoff.

00:01:20.079 --> 00:01:24.620
Then the absolute chaos that ensues when the
infected start to swarm everyone is an action

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:26.120
wet dream.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:30.050
Just having dozens of people laying into a
horde of zombies with assault rifles is metal

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:34.850
as hell and further throws the excitement
factor of this scene into the heavens.

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:38.579
Along with this portraying the infected as
extremely powerful and menacing.

00:01:38.579 --> 00:01:41.509
That’s something that I love about the show
so far.

00:01:41.509 --> 00:01:45.590
The writers have been very particular as to
when they have scenes with the infected so

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:47.810
that they can preserve their intimidation.

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:51.780
Because if you have too many scenes of Joel
killing clickers then you start to lose the

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:56.440
impact and scare factor of the undead since
they don’t pose much of a threat anymore.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:01.160
Like the few scenes we have so far with them
have all been terrifying and our characters

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:03.409
are always on the verge of death.

00:02:03.409 --> 00:02:07.880
This feeling of no plot armor is outstanding
and feels rare nowadays.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:11.660
Because it’s really hard to realistically
raise the danger while not compromising the

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:17.550
logic in having your characters actually survive
without the intervention of the writer gods.

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:21.410
The only aspect within this action setpiece
that relates to this point that partially

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:24.840
bothered me was the fact that Joel never missed
the clickers.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:29.660
All of these shots seemed pretty hard to execute,
especially since he was having trouble earlier,

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:34.290
but I can buy it because it’s definitely
possible for Joel to succeed here.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:37.980
Going back to some of the things I loved about
this scene is the editing.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:41.819
If you think about it we have 3 different
perspectives running around this moshpit of

00:02:41.819 --> 00:02:46.610
clickers and at no point does it ever feel
jarring or incoherent.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:50.240
Every shot feels very purposeful as to the
flow of the scene.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:54.370
An example of this is Joel shooting a clicker
that’s approaching Ellie and Henry which

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:56.530
then pairs their two perspectives together.

00:02:56.530 --> 00:03:00.959
Now the edit is primarily focusing on telling
their story until getting to a good stopping

00:03:00.959 --> 00:03:03.340
point in their micro journey through the scene.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:07.820
Like when Ellie gets into the car and achieves
her temporary goal of getting to shelter.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:11.381
Then we cut to some brief shots of people
getting wrecked to increase the panic, which

00:03:11.381 --> 00:03:15.319
is followed by the next micro story beat like
with the bloater emerging.

00:03:15.319 --> 00:03:19.360
Pacing wise this is perfect and I just love
this structure throughout the scene.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:23.360
Then another clever thing is that the editor
chooses to ramp up the number of cuts whenever

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:25.530
our characters are in mortal danger.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:29.390
This form of editing increases the anxiety
of the viewer and further makes you relate

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:31.240
to the state of panic that our characters
are enduring.

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:32.240
It’s nice that the entire scene isn’t
quickly cut though and is primarily reserved

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:33.240
for these moments.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.709
And then some of the other shots we are allowed
to view for a longer duration.

00:03:35.709 --> 00:03:39.720
Like I just love this wide shot of the clickers
initially pouring out of the sinkhole and

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:41.709
witnessing all of the destruction.

00:03:41.709 --> 00:03:46.290
It gives you a great grasp as to the insane
scale of the scene and it creates a first

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:49.250
impression of being stunned by what’s transpiring.

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:52.829
Also while on the subject of editing, there
was this one shot of Perry where it’s his

00:03:52.829 --> 00:03:54.930
reaction shot to the bloater yelling.

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:59.390
The editor here opts to slow down the footage
but since they probably shot it at 24 frames

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:03.310
a second, it becomes a quick slideshow and
it is very noticeable.

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:08.209
It just seems a little odd to slow it down
here in an otherwise really well-edited scene.

00:04:08.209 --> 00:04:11.440
Because if they didn’t shoot it at a very
high frame rate that actually allows you to

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:15.900
slow down footage given the extra frames,
then it sticks out like a sore thumb.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.940
Looking at some other key moments from this
scene, I love how the bloater is introduced

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:21.240
here in the show.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:25.620
Because in the game, it was introduced on
completely different terms and also much earlier

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:26.620
in the story.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:30.140
I would talk about it a bit, but I feel like
that would just be spoilers for the game at

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:31.140
this point.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:35.110
However, the terms in which it’s introduced
in the game I don’t think is as crazy or

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:37.500
as grand as it is here in the show.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:42.020
The execution of the bloater was perfect and
the entire essence of him being like a leader

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.820
of sorts reminded me of the Night King during
Hardhome.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:48.949
The sound design of his basey roars were chilling
and the fact that they brought in the death

00:04:48.949 --> 00:04:53.270
animations into the show where he ripped Perry’s
head off was a nice touch.

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:56.830
Another standout moment to me was the child
clicker in the car with Ellie.

00:04:56.830 --> 00:05:00.509
Like you think that the scene couldn’t get
even more tense and then we have this little

00:05:00.509 --> 00:05:04.699
isolated encounter as this demented clicker
is folding over seats like a noodle.

00:05:04.699 --> 00:05:09.550
Then the fact that this same clicker ends
up killing Kathleen is both a great action

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:10.830
and writing moment.

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:14.340
It’s deeply ironic how she was threatening
and wanting to kill the kids to then getting

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:15.680
killed by one.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:19.120
Along with her ignoring the festering issue
of the clickers in the tunnels in the pursuit

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:22.080
of Henry so it ends up biting her in the ass.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.009
Narratively I think Kathleen was done great
justice in this episode.

00:05:26.009 --> 00:05:30.270
She wasn’t very compelling in the one prior
but through her backstory of finding out that

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:33.770
her brother was originally the leader and
that responsibility was kind of passed on

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:35.740
to her makes sense.

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:39.270
Along with her essentially fitting the mold
of being a ruthless leader and not being afraid

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:40.550
of killing people.

00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:44.169
It just made her first scene feel a little
contradictory where she’s breaking up when

00:05:44.169 --> 00:05:48.580
faced with killing this person she has a minor
connection to, to then condemning all of these

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:49.580
people to die.

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:53.030
I know she doesn’t have to kill them herself,
but she’s still the one who’s ordering

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:54.030
it.

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:57.240
Also, this moment where she’s trying to
question these prisoners feels like she’s

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:01.630
speaking with the same cadence of a kindergarten
teacher addressing their class when they did

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:10.449
something bad.

00:06:10.449 --> 00:06:15.259
So I still wish they made Kathleen a bit more
intimidating because the writing surrounding

00:06:15.259 --> 00:06:18.620
her character is really solid in the larger
context.

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:22.949
Like giving a perspective to this group was
a great way to have a better action confrontation

00:06:22.949 --> 00:06:26.660
at the end of this episode, and one that had
significantly more payoff.

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:30.490
That’s one of the biggest improvements so
far from the video game.

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:34.509
I know some people were annoyed that the last
episode had to do some actual build-up that

00:06:34.509 --> 00:06:38.520
requires time, but I think it was definitely
worth it in the long run.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:42.569
And the idea that people were floating that
these two episodes should have been combined

00:06:42.569 --> 00:06:45.389
I don’t think would have worked for two
main reasons.

00:06:45.389 --> 00:06:49.889
Firstly, the last episode had a great endpoint
for Joel and Ellie’s relationship, and Henry

00:06:49.889 --> 00:06:52.430
showing up was a good cliffhanger opportunity.

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:56.370
The second reason has to do with showing Henry’s
perspective prior to meeting Joel.

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:59.990
If you were to jump to this flashback right
after this confrontation it would have felt

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:03.930
much more jarring than having it separated
by two different episodes.

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:08.479
Because episode 5 is more about Henry and
Sam than it is about Joel and Ellie.

00:07:08.479 --> 00:07:12.710
Joel and Ellie are still definitely prominent,
but the writers opt to give more development

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:17.830
to Henry and Sam so that their depressing
and heartbreaking demise hits even harder.

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:22.010
Like you may identify their opening scenes
as not really being integral to the plot,

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:25.740
but I think they were necessary in setting
up the bond between these two brothers.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:30.250
The scene in the attic where Henry tries to
brighten up sam’s mood was very wholesome

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:32.120
and instantly makes you love them.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:36.700
I adore how the music here is able to flourish
uninterrupted audio-wise because these two

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:39.090
characters are communicating via signing.

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:43.070
It allows the music to resonate more with
you and deaf characters I feel like have to

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:48.639
rely a lot more on facial and bodily performances
which is the root of acting and I love that.

00:07:48.639 --> 00:07:53.300
Making Sam a deaf character was a brilliant
change and as they said in the behind-the-scenes,

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:56.099
this aspect makes Sam even more reliant on
Henry.

00:07:56.099 --> 00:08:00.340
It’s a quick and efficient way to bring
them closer together and it works with no

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:01.340
faults.

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:05.009
Although, I wouldn’t have minded if they
leaned a bit more on Sam’s deafness within

00:08:05.009 --> 00:08:06.729
a zombie apocalypse.

00:08:06.729 --> 00:08:10.490
Like maybe having a moment where he’s approached
by a clicker without him noticing and having

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:12.610
to be saved by Henry or something.

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:17.150
But regardless, Sam and Henry were still flawless
throughout the whole episode.

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:21.819
Not even in terms of writing because they
both did an incredible job in the acting department.

00:08:21.819 --> 00:08:25.789
Lamar Johnson as Henry in particular really
blew me away.

00:08:25.789 --> 00:08:30.540
Every scene he was in he was very charismatic
and he always had this air of innocence to

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:31.540
him.

00:08:31.540 --> 00:08:35.789
He’s very honest and genuine and Lamar Jonson
has a warm factor that exudes from him in

00:08:35.789 --> 00:08:37.459
almost every moment.

00:08:37.459 --> 00:08:41.680
He just comes across as so likable and relatable
to the point that you are always sympathizing

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:43.190
with what he has to say.

00:08:43.190 --> 00:08:47.860
Also, the writers even gave Henry more of
a backstory here to further humanize him.

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:52.269
Expanding Henry’s backstory and entangling
it with Kathleen’s is much more interesting

00:08:52.269 --> 00:08:56.490
than what happens in the game because there
it was basically just raiders driving out

00:08:56.490 --> 00:08:57.530
Henry’s group.

00:08:57.530 --> 00:09:01.610
However, in the show, the writers make it
to where Henry has to make the choice to either

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:05.779
rat out Kathleen’s brother that leads to
his death or choose to get some essential

00:09:05.779 --> 00:09:07.500
medicine for his brother.

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:10.649
This was very smart and doesn’t compromise
his character.

00:09:10.649 --> 00:09:14.590
Because anyone in this situation would choose
to protect their younger brother and on the

00:09:14.590 --> 00:09:18.830
flip side, if someone was responsible for
your brother’s death you’d rightfully

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:20.269
want them to pay.

00:09:20.269 --> 00:09:24.600
This makes for a good parallel between these
two characters and is surprising that Craig

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:28.450
Mazin basically came up with this on the fly
from how deep this is.

00:09:28.450 --> 00:09:32.810
I just love how in almost every inside the
episode, Neil Druckman is constantly like

00:09:32.810 --> 00:09:37.410
ugghhh I wish I wrote that into the game and
is constantly praising Craig.

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:41.171
This kind of bond between the original writer
loving the work of the new writer and not

00:09:41.171 --> 00:09:45.640
being overly protective is a great pairing
that further leads to an unbelievable result

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:46.829
so far.

00:09:46.829 --> 00:09:50.660
Along with giving Neil Druckman the ability
to revisit his past work and make it even

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:55.980
better since he’s had a lot of time to reflect
on it and think of what can be improved.

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:59.690
Before going into the final scene, I’ll
highlight a few other of my favorite moments

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:04.500
to delay the inevitable sadness that comes
with talking about the tragic ending.

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:08.240
One moment that made me burst out laughing
was Joel’s asshole voice moment.

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:12.620
It really comes out of nowhere with how funny
it is and is even better when Joel says something

00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:15.529
else that shows the fact that he’s still
really tense.

00:10:15.529 --> 00:10:20.180
And then one last moment that I’ll highlight
is the gang finding the underground classroom.

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:25.010
This scene has a lot to offer because it allows
the innocence of Ellie to briefly resurface

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:28.290
because, at the end of the day, she’s still
a kid.

00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:32.970
This aspect about her character coming out
is really nice to see because Ellie is a naturally

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:38.380
tough character and she is able to express
herself from a more whimsical internal perspective.

00:10:38.380 --> 00:10:42.260
Which this allows some great bonding moments
with Sam to make his death that much more

00:10:42.260 --> 00:10:44.730
sad for Ellie and him.

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:47.150
Along with this whole classroom setting being
interesting.

00:10:47.150 --> 00:10:51.320
I wonder if this group was initially in the
tunnels but they got royally screwed over

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:56.010
by FEDRA bringing the undead down there and
that was the reason for their downfall.

00:10:56.010 --> 00:11:00.480
This scene also allowed for Joel and Henry
to reach a better status in their brief relationship

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:04.480
that was really integral for Joel in being
upset by Henry’s death.

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:08.130
This scene does a lot the more you think about
it while also offering a good beat in the

00:11:08.130 --> 00:11:12.019
pacing to slow down before the crazy action
set piece.

00:11:12.019 --> 00:11:16.850
So let’s finally get into the emotional
despair of the ending of the episode.

00:11:16.850 --> 00:11:21.980
Henry reacting to Sam being infected was a
perfect blend of confusion, upset, and utter

00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:26.730
shock that it would seem impossible to act
out this moment from how complicated the emotional

00:11:26.730 --> 00:11:28.480
requirement is here.

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:33.300
But Lamar Johnson blows this scene out of
the water and so does everyone else.

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:37.880
Joel and Ellie equally match this and it just
creates a magnificent scene that guts the

00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:39.519
audience emotionally.

00:11:39.519 --> 00:11:43.829
Just this entire plot point is so devastating
because finally Joel and Ellie have found

00:11:43.829 --> 00:11:47.750
a pairing that they’ve come to enjoy but
is ripped away from them because of the nature

00:11:47.750 --> 00:11:49.260
of the world.

00:11:49.260 --> 00:11:53.250
That and Ellie trying to heal Sam was fucking
depressing and you immediately know that’s

00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:57.860
not how science works and the fate of Sam
is locked in from that point on.

00:11:57.860 --> 00:12:02.010
Even playing through the game I still feel
floored by what happened because of how amazing

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:04.050
the writing and acting is here.

00:12:04.050 --> 00:12:08.080
Then the contrast between Joel and Ellie is
further explored here in the show where you

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:12.370
see Joel expressing more sorrow towards the
two brothers while Ellie is quick to move

00:12:12.370 --> 00:12:13.370
on.

00:12:13.370 --> 00:12:16.529
It’s an opposite kind of trajectory for
the two characters as Joel learns to open

00:12:16.529 --> 00:12:21.220
up more to others and be more caring while
Ellie is learning more from Joel in that she

00:12:21.220 --> 00:12:24.640
needs to be more resilient to survive in this
hostile world.

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:28.709
I think this moment in their relationship
was a great little beat and seeing it expressed

00:12:28.709 --> 00:12:32.990
through this shot of Joel in the foreground
strucken by grief contrasted by Ellie far

00:12:32.990 --> 00:12:36.990
away in the background out of focus was a
clever move by the DP.

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:41.370
God just every aspect about this show is so
great to the point that watching anything

00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:43.180
else feels like an immediate downgrade.

00:12:43.180 --> 00:12:47.300
Thank you for watching and let me know what
you thought about Episode 5 of The Last of

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:47.830
Us.

